Security is a fundamental engineering principle of Amonite.

Rather than relying primarily on additional security software, the project seeks to improve security through deliberate engineering decisions, careful system integration and sensible defaults built upon the Debian platform.

> **Every default is an intentional decision.**

## Supported Releases

Security fixes are provided only for releases that are currently supported by the project.

Pre-release versions (Alpha, Beta and Release Candidate) are intended for evaluation and testing. They may contain known issues and should not be considered suitable for production environments.

The current support status of each release is published on the official website.

## Reporting a Vulnerability

If you believe you have discovered a security vulnerability in Amonite, please report it responsibly.

Please include:

- a clear description of the issue;
- affected release(s);
- steps to reproduce the problem;
- expected and observed behaviour;
- any relevant logs or technical information.

Please avoid publicly disclosing vulnerabilities until they have been evaluated.

## Security Principles

Security-related engineering decisions follow a small number of principles.

### Platform First

Whenever practical, Amonite builds upon Debian's mature security mechanisms before introducing project-specific solutions.

### Least Complexity

Reducing unnecessary software, services and custom infrastructure generally improves auditability and reduces long-term maintenance.

### Transparency

Security mechanisms should remain understandable.

Users should be able to inspect, configure and reason about the behaviour of their systems.

### Continuous Evaluation

Security decisions are periodically reviewed as Debian and the surrounding ecosystem evolve.

Better approaches may replace existing implementations whenever they provide measurable improvements while remaining consistent with the project's engineering principles.

## Scope

Amonite is responsible for the engineering decisions introduced by the project.

Security issues originating in upstream software, Debian packages or third-party components are generally addressed through their respective maintainers, although the project may provide updated releases when appropriate.

## Disclosure

The project follows a coordinated disclosure process whenever practical.

Reported vulnerabilities are evaluated, reproduced when possible and addressed according to their impact on supported releases.

Security-related changes are documented in the corresponding release notes whenever appropriate.

## Contact

Security reports may be submitted through the project's official communication channels listed at:

https://amonite.org

Please avoid reporting security vulnerabilities through public issue trackers whenever possible.
